\section{Introduction: The Autogenerative Hypothesis}
% ============================================================
Language, cognition, and natural computation exhibit an intrinsic property of \emph{autogeneration}: each moment of activity encodes the conditions for its own continuation. The concept, articulated by Barenholtz~(2025), redefines autoregression as more than a statistical convenience; it is the very mode of natural self-propagation. In contrast to Markovian processes, where the next state depends only on the current one, autogenerative systems draw upon the entire trajectory of prior states. They are inherently \emph{non-Markovian}, operating on the accumulated curvature of experience rather than on instantaneous state vectors.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This view renders the corpus of language not merely as data but as a \emph{field of continuation rules}â€”a statistical manifold whose internal topology determines its own dynamics. In such a manifold, the ``pregnant present'' is a boundary condition that enfolds its history and projects its own future trajectory. Autogeneration therefore serves as the bridge between memory, inference, and meaning. The goal of this section is to formalize that bridge using information geometry, category theory, and dynamical-systems analysis.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">% ============================================================
\section{Mathematical Framework of Autogenerative Systems}
% ============================================================
\subsection{Definition of Autogeneration}
Let ( \mathcal{S} = (X, \mathcal{T}) ) denote a sequence-generating system where ( X ) is the embedding space of tokens or states, and ( \mathcal{T}: X^* \to \Delta(X) ) is a continuation operator mapping a finite history to a probability distribution over the next state. The system is \emph{autogenerative} if ( \mathcal{T} ) is determined entirely by the internal geometry of ( X ) and by the statistics of its trajectories. Formally,
[
\mathcal{T}(x_{1:t}) = F_\theta(x_{1:t}), \quad \text{with } \theta \text{ inferred solely from sequential data.} $$
No external syntactic or semantic rules are imposed. The corpus itself provides the sufficient structure for generation.</p>
